1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
 In this video, I'm going to talk about the third and final type of engineering

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,920
 polymer. It's not quite a thermoplastic and it's not quite a thermo set.

3
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:11,040
 Though it is probably a little bit closer to the second than the first.

4
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:14,160
 This type of plastic is called an elastomer.

5
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:17,760
 Elastomers, as the name suggests, are very elastic.

6
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:21,120
 Unlike thermoplastics, which don't have any crosslinks at all.

7
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:24,400
 And unlike thermo sets, which have many, many crosslinks that

8
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:27,200
 make them quite stiff, but also a little bit brittle.

9
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:31,200
 Elastomers have a very finely tuned amount of crosslinks,

10
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:36,320
 reasonably few crosslinks that allow them to unravel like coiled up springs

11
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:39,520
 and provide some sort of restoring force, without experiencing

12
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:44,400
 visco-elasticity, without experiencing plastic slip of chains.

13
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:48,800
 True to their namesake, Elastomers are typically used in circumstances that require

14
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:53,040
 elasticity, that require a little bit of flexibility, a little bit of give.

15
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:58,000
 circumstances like gaskets and earrings and seals and rubbers and tires.

16
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,080
 And elastomers are really good at this.

17
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:03,520
 Some of them being able to stretch 700% their original length

18
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:06,240
 elastically, without plastically deforming.

19
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:09,360
 Some of the earliest elastomers were made from natural products.

20
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:13,920
 A natural rubber, for example, was made by vulcanizing or crosslinking

21
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:17,840
 latex, which is a product from the rubber tree, the sap of the rubber tree.

22
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:20,800
 And the amount of crosslinking, the amount of vulcanization,

23
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:26,160
 has for a long time been controlled by the amount of sulfur that is added to this latex.

24
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:30,400
 These days, natural rubber products have largely been succeeded by synthetic rubbers,

25
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:33,120
 rubbers not made from the rubber tree sap.

26
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:37,760
 And this is because synthetic rubbers, like the most popular one, styrene butadiene,

27
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:41,120
 allow us to carefully control and chew the properties.

28
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:43,520
 I'm not going to talk about every elastomer that's out there,

29
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:48,560
 but I will introduce you to one more family, the family of silicone elastomers.

30
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:52,960
 Silicone elastomers, as the name suggests, are largely composed of silicon.

31
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:57,120
 The backbone of a silicone polymer alternates between silicon and oxygen atoms,

32
00:01:57,120 --> 00:01:58,880
 and these serve an important purpose.

33
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:03,280
 The inorganic nature of this backbone means that silicone generally have much higher operating

34
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:04,880
 temperatures than other polymers.

35
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:09,280
 And partly owing to this, but also owing to the liquid repelling properties of these

36
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:14,560
 polymers due to the combination of their inorganic backbone and their usually organic fingers,

37
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:18,800
 means that they make really, really good gaskets and seals and o-rings.

38
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:21,520
 I'm going to leave it there for elastomers, and I'm conscious that I haven't

39
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:24,320
 dealt particularly deep here for the same reasons that I didn't

40
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:27,280
 dealt particularly deep when we were talking about thermosets.

41
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:30,480
 If you're going to be looking for an elastomer to solve a particular problem,

42
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:35,360
 the type of elastomer you choose is largely going to be a function of the specifics of that problem.

43
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:39,600
 What I encourage you to do, just like with thermosets, is a little bit of independent research.

44
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:42,720
 Have a look at edge packet, the different types of elastomers that are documented,

45
